  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances decrease today \nbefore increasing Thursday and Friday as a cold front crosses.\n\nLooking at a lull in the thunderstorm activity today with subsidence \nleft behind the caravan of MCSs that traversed the region over the \nlast 24 to 48 hours. Overall flow remains out of the west and \nnorthwest which is allowing slightly drier and much more stable air \nto advect into the region. After a few light showers (mainly \nBaltimore/east) and little patchy fog this morning, expect a spotty \nshower or thunderstorm this afternoon, mainly as a result of terrain \ncirculations or the bay/river breeze. Skies will trend partly cloudy \nto mostly sunny with abundant high cloud cover and even a little \nwildfire smoke from fires burning over the Pacific Northwest/Canada. \nThe suspended smoke and high clouds will help limit destabilization \nthis afternoon with a focus, mainly south and west of our region. \n\nThe quiet weather doesn't last too long as we monitor two shortwaves \nset to track across the region. The first shortwave looks to cross \nlater this evening into Thursday morning (9pm-2am) with another \nalong the actual cold front Friday. Both of these disturbances will \nallow for an uptick of shower and thunderstorm chances across the \nregion. Timing and coverage remain a bit uncertain yielding some \nquestions in regards to the overall severe threat. Instability and \nshear do look to increase a bit with MLCAPE values around 1000-1500 \nj/kg and shear values up to 36 kts tonight into Friday. Machine \nlearning/AI guidance remains mixed on severe probabilities generally \nranging between 5-20 percent with a focus across southwestern \nportions of the forecast area. If severe weather were to occur, \ndamaging winds and isolated instances of flash flooding would be the \nprimary concerns.  \n\nOutside of thunderstorms, look for seasonable temperatures and \nincreased humidity, especially Thursday and Friday ahead of the \nfrontal boundary. Highs will range from the mid 80s to low 90s (70s \nmountains) with overnight lows in the mid 60s and low 70s (upper 50s \nand low 60s mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Dry conditions Saturday, with chances for showers \nand thunderstorms Sunday and Monday.\n\nHigh pressure will build to our north on Saturday. Northerly winds \nwill usher cooler and drier air into the area, with dewpoints \ndropping into the 50s and lower 60s, and temperatures only making it \ninto the upper 70s to mid 80s. Conditions will remain dry beneath \nmostly sunny skies.\n\nHigh pressure will progress offshore on Sunday as low pressure and \nan associated shortwave track through the Great Lakes. Winds will \nturn out of the south in response, drawing deeper moisture back \nnorth into the forecast area. Depending on the track of that system \nand how much moisture return occurs ahead of it, showers and \nthunderstorms may be possible for portions of the forecast area on \nSunday afternoon and evening. Additional showers and thunderstorms \nmay be possible again on Monday, before drier air works back into \nthe area on Tuesday.",
